2bb1a31 (sbobyn/three-avbd main, 2026-10-12)

Local patches, all worth reporting upstream: each says where it is, what it does, why, and how to
see it (repro). The repros marked "lab" are cases of three-breakable's scripts/solver-lab.mjs: each
builds a world of its own with nothing but three-avbd's World, so it can be copied into a page to
run against upstream (in three-breakable, `node scripts/solver-lab.mjs --only <case>` runs it).
Upstream has none of the switches below; "off" is a switch turned off here, which is upstream's
behaviour unless said. Lab numbers are from 2026-10-16 (RTX 4090, Linux, Chrome; the GPU shared).
The tests-gpu files follow every patch, but couldn't be run here (Dawn won't load under Node on this
machine); docs/API.md describes the new options.

These eleven are every change from upstream: diffed against a clean checkout of 2bb1a31 on
2026-10-16, after three-breakable's phase E (which changed nothing here), the only other files that
differ are docs/API.md, tests/radii.test.ts and four tests-gpu files (avbd3d-gpu, grid-levels,
lib, manifold-substeps), each following these; upstream's docs/media, public/paintings and
tests/fixtures aren't vendored.

- Slots wait a step: src/lib/world.ts (World.parked, add, remove, step).
  What: a removed body's slot is given to a new body only after a step has run with the old one
  parked in it (a full world still takes a parked slot at once).
  Why: reused in the same frame, the new body took over the old one's warm starts (the narrowphase
  matches last step's pair of the same two slots: its forces, penalties and static-friction
  anchors), and the contact watch read the pair as still touching, so the new body's landing was
  never reported. It replaces an earlier patch for a body removed before its first flush (freed at
  once, two bodies were queued for one slot and every later handle was one off: seen as frozen
  rubble jumping to the parking spot).
  Repro: heavy boxes resting on the ground, removed and replaced in the same frame by light ones
  reporting contacts (or loose hulls removed and cut into eight cells each, made in the freed
  slots): read back after a step, the new bodies started up to 8 mm and 0.8 m/s off where they
  were made, and one made touching the ground, as the old one did, gave no contact begin.
  Tests: tests-gpu/lib.gpu.test.ts ("removed bodies free their slots for new ones").
- The broadphase grid in levels: src/avbd3d/gpu (radii.ts, solver.ts uploadStatics,
  wgsl-collision.ts findPairs/findFixedLarge, layout.ts Params).
  What: the small bodies go in up to MAX_LEVELS (8) levels of cells, each twice the size of the one
  below, from twice a typical radius (the movers' median, when below the median of all) up to twice
  the largest small one; a body in the finest level whose cells are at least twice its radius. A
  mover probes its own level and every coarser one, each only in the cells that can hold an
  overlapping body (2 x 2 x 2 for a body much smaller than the cells, never more than the old
  3 x 3 x 3); fixed bodies probe the coarser levels holding movers (a bitmask) as findFixedLarge did
  the one coarse level. The same pairs; a single size of body still makes one level.
  Why: one level's cells were twice the largest small radius, and with more than MAX_LARGE (64) big
  bodies the 65th sized them: a terrain of ~1,100 fixed cliff hulls made 32 m cells, so every small
  moving piece walked hundreds of bodies in its 27 cells (and findFixedLarge, each fixed body
  thousands).
  Repro: small moving pieces among more than 64 fixed bodies much bigger than them (three-breakable's
  Sun Temple, ~1,100 cliff hulls: collision 1.8 ms a step with a few loose pieces, 2-3 ms with a
  blast's ~150; now 0.26-0.38 and ~1.1. Brick Street ~2 ms, now ~0.6-1).
  Tests: tests/radii.test.ts (the levels), tests-gpu/grid-levels.gpu.test.ts (the pairs against
  brute force on mixed scenes of 5 cm to 40 m bodies, sleeping, waking and resizing).
- A speed limit: src/avbd3d/gpu (solver.ts GpuParams3D.maxSpeed, wgsl-solve.ts withinReach,
  mostPush, evalContact, updateVelocities, layout.ts Params limits) and src/lib/world.ts
  (World.maxSpeed).
  What: an optional limit on a moving body's speed (default Infinity: as upstream). With it, (1)
  every primal update keeps the body within maxSpeed·dt of where it started the step, and the
  velocity is derived from that pose (they agree); (2) no contact pushes harder than the pair's
  reduced mass times maxSpeed over dt (a push cut so stiffens the step only as much as is left of
  it, and doesn't ramp its penalty). The first version only slowed a body to maxSpeed at the end of
  the step.
  Why: a light body jammed between heavy or fixed ones can't satisfy both contacts, and the dual
  raised their forces every iteration (to thousands of times its weight): their mismatch threw it
  at tens of m/s, spinning, and, its velocity alone clamped, kilometres in one step, dragging what it
  was jointed or pressed to. This bounds such a piece at maxSpeed; it doesn't stop a pinched piece
  being shot out at up to it (two opposed pushes, each within the cap, can still differ by it).
  The price: a light body holds at most about maxSpeed / (dt·g) times its weight (245 at 40 m/s and
  60 steps a second); under more it's pressed into what it rests on.
  Repro: no small one yet. The flings were traced by stepping a collapse by hand (three-breakable's
  Neon Alley, every other step a substep) and reading every body after every step: 0.1-20 g pieces
  (a neon tube fragment between the street and a fallen pane, a glass shard between two signboxes,
  a steel mullion under a 4 t slab) carried 40 to 3,000 units of force from both sides and left at
  10-40 m/s spinning at 100 rad/s, up to 10^13 m off in a step. Over 500 such steps, steps that sped
  a piece up by 8 m/s or more away from any blast fell from 14 to 5 with this and the substep patch
  (below). The lab's pinch case (slivers under a dropped slab, chips in a V under a dropped block,
  panes between boxes driven together) doesn't show it: 4.5 m/s gained at most between readbacks
  with no limit, 1.2 with it. The lab's stack case shows the price: a 0.3 m box of density 1 under
  one of 2,500 is pressed 0.29 m into the ground at 40 m/s, 1 cm with no limit.
- Friction where a contact slips: src/avbd3d/gpu (GpuParams3D.slipFriction, FLAG_SLIP_FRICTION,
  wgsl-solve.ts accumulate and dualManifold). On by default.
  What: rows cut to the friction cone keep only as much of their stiffness in the Hessian as the
  cut left (floored at the pair's own m/dt² where they were stiffer), and a contact that slips
  clears its STICK_BIT.
  Why: upstream (and the reference) keep the full penalty, ramped while the contact stuck: for a
  light body (m/dt² under the penalty) the Newton step then hardly felt the clamped friction force,
  and it slid on as if on ice. And the stick bit, set only while within the cone, survived sliding,
  so a contact that had stuck kept its anchors and, once stopped, pulled the body back towards them.
  Without the floor, a light cube sliding pitched over its front edge and bounced along (58 m).
  Repro: lab slide (0.3 m boxes of density 0.001 to 1000 sliding from 10 m/s on friction 0.5, where
  v²/(2μg) is 10.2 m; then the same pushed after resting 2 s). Off: density 0.001 went 36.8 m and
  density 10 13.4 m; pushed after resting, density 0.1 and 1 slid 10.1 m and were pulled back to
  2.3 and 5.6 m. On: 10.1-11.3 m, none pulled back.
  Tests: reference comparisons switch it off (tests-gpu/avbd3d-gpu.gpu.test.ts), as they do the
  three below. The stick bit is the reference's too.
- Deep penetration pushed out: src/avbd3d/gpu (GpuParams3D.pushOut, FLAG_PUSH_OUT, wgsl-solve.ts
  pushOut, layout.ts PUSH_SLOP, PUSH_SPEED). On by default.
  What: a contact's penetration deeper than 1 cm past the margin is pushed out at up to 1 m/s,
  besides stabilization's 1 - alpha a step.
  Why: stabilization takes 1% of it a step (alpha 0.99), so a piece pressed 20 cm into the ground (by
  a load, or by a step that found the touch late) was still in it seconds later, and came to rest
  there.
  Repro: a 0.3 m box (density 500) made 20 cm into a fixed ground box, at rest. Off: its bottom is
  17 cm under after 0.25 s and 11 cm after 1 s. On: out by 0.25 s.
- Speculative contacts: src/avbd3d/gpu (GpuParams3D.speculative, FLAG_SPECULATIVE,
  wgsl-collision.ts speculation, faceSeparated, testPair, probeLevel, testAxis, faceManifold,
  collide, narrowphase; wgsl-hull.ts collidePoly, hullFaceContact; wgsl-solve.ts evalContact) and
  src/lib/contacts.ts. On by default.
  What: a moving body's pairs and contacts with fixed bodies are found while they're apart, as far
  as it goes in three steps (SPECULATIVE_STEPS), but no further than the grid cells next to the
  mover's own (probeLevel: against the large bodies the whole way, against small fixed ones about a
  cell). Such a contact holds no force until the gap closes (the normal row takes the gap past the
  margin whole, not 1 - alpha of it). Between two moving bodies, contacts are made on touch, as
  before. The face bias (FLAG_FACE_BIAS: an edge axis must beat the face's separation by 5% of it and
  1 cm) is taken on the separation's size, so it holds for pairs apart too. The contact watch follows:
  a pair begins touching once a point has met (gap within the margin) or pushed, and a begin's
  impulse is the larger of the step's and their speed into each other as the step began times their
  reduced mass (a speculative contact takes a hit over two steps). COLLISION_MARGIN is exported from
  layout.ts.
  Why: found only once they overlapped, a thin piece thrown at the ground at 30 m/s was half a metre
  into a 1 m ground box by then, past its middle, and was pushed out of its far side. Scaled by 0.95
  as before, a positive gap let an edge win the face bias, and a flat plate falling onto the ground
  met it on one edge point, tipped and went 7 cm in.
  Repro: lab throw (120 plates and slivers, 1-3 cm thick, thrown at a 1 m ground box at 10-40 m/s,
  every other step a substep): off, 41 through; on, 1. Lab landing (a 1 kg box falling 1.25 m,
  m·v 4.95 N·s): one begin, 5.07 N·s off, 4.74 on.
  Tests: tests-gpu/manifold-substeps.gpu.test.ts ("speculative contacts: a thin plate thrown at the
  ground at 30 m/s stops on it"; the substep tests there turn it off).
- Sliding over seams: src/avbd3d/gpu (GpuParams3D.seams, FLAG_SEAMS, wgsl-collision.ts rides,
  testAxis, collide, narrowphase; wgsl-hull.ts queryFaces, collidePoly, polyFace). On by default.
  What: a moving body level with a fixed body's upward face (overlapping it along its normal by no
  more than twice the margin, the face within 45 degrees of up) meets that face, not any axis that
  separates them more. Only for a moving and a fixed body, and only upward faces: a thin plate
  sliding into the foot of a wall still meets the wall.
  Why: a piece sliding on one fixed body rests the margin into it, so its bottom is that far below a
  flush neighbour's top, and the neighbour's side was the SAT's axis: a contact there (found ahead by
  speculation, or as the piece crossed into the neighbour by less than the margin) stopped it dead
  at the seam or threw it up. Without speculation, at a few seams in a hundred; with it, at every
  one. Report it with speculative contacts.
  Repro: lab seams (boxes of 5-40 cm and a hull slid over a floor of 0.5 m fixed box or hull tiles at
  3-15 m/s, friction 0.4). Both off: 0.51-1.33 of v²/(2μg), hopping at up to 1.26 m/s. Speculative
  alone: 0.01-0.76, at up to 3.28 m/s. Both on: 0.98-1.10, at most 0.15 m/s.
  Tests: tests-gpu/manifold-substeps.gpu.test.ts ("seams: a box slides over flush fixed tiles as far
  as friction takes it, without a hop").
- Substeps collide again: src/avbd3d/gpu/solver.ts step (reuseManifolds), wgsl-collision.ts.
  What: a substep (step({ reuseManifolds: true })) keeps the detecting step's broadphase pairs and
  runs the narrowphase on them again (pairs of bodies that haven't moved keep their points, as on
  any step, reuseContacts); the kernel that copied every manifold whole (reuseManifolds) is gone.
  Why: copied, a light piece spun half a turn since met its contacts at anchors and normals far from
  where it was, and was flung; and a substep found no new contact, so what fell into the ground in
  one went in.
  Repro: the hand-stepped collapse in the speed limit's repro, every other step a substep: every
  kilometre jump traced was in a substep, and the same 700 steps with every step detecting had none.
  With substeps as before, 57% of the pieces' first sinkings into the ground were in substeps. It
  costs the narrowphase on a slow frame's later substeps (0.3-0.5 ms each there).
  Tests: tests-gpu/manifold-substeps.gpu.test.ts follows (the reuse tests).
- Rolling resistance: src/avbd3d/gpu (wgsl-solve.ts rolled, updateVelocities, warmStartBodies;
  layout.ts Body initialPos.w; solver.ts pack), ref/body.ts (Rigid.rolling) and src/lib/world.ts
  (BodyOptions.rolling, Body.rolling).
  What: rolling resistance per body (default 0: as upstream): a torque against its turn of the
  coefficient times the push its contacts carry (their last iteration's) times its smallest half
  width, applied where the step's spin is derived. Nothing in the air.
  Why: nothing in the contacts resists turning, so a piece rolling over its faces (a dropped drum,
  a baluster) loses little and rolls on.
  Repro: lab roll (a 16-sided drum 0.6 m across set rolling at 2 m/s; a cube spinning in the air).
  The drum goes 3.57 m at 0 and 2.86 m at 0.02; the cube spins at 4.758 rad/s after 1 s either way.
- Scan pipelines once per device: src/avbd2d/gpu/scan.ts.
  What: the prefix scan's shader module and pipelines are made once per device and shared.
  Why: GpuSolver3D makes a scan again on every addBodies (its length changes): a shader module and
  two pipelines each time.
  Repro: add bodies in batches over a few frames and count GPUDevice.createComputePipeline: two more
  each batch before (remade 122 times in one of three-breakable's collapses), none after the first.
- The contact-event pass made at subscription: src/lib/world.ts (onContact, contactPass).
  What: the contact-event pass is made when a listener subscribes, not when the first body reports
  contacts.
  Why: a scene subscribes as it loads; made when the first body was set to report contacts (in
  three-breakable, the first piece to come loose), its two pipelines compiled in the middle of play.
  Repro: subscribe with onContact, step a while, then set reportContacts on a body: before, the next
  step's flush made the pass (a 50-67 ms frame here); now nothing is made then.
